+The editable `BeatGrid` model is JUCE-independent. It validates a detected grid, permits transactional beat-zero and segment-BPM correction, supports bounded variable-tempo segments inserted at exact beat positions, preserves continuous beat numbering and converts beat↔time in both directions. `PerformanceDeckOwner` consumes only reviewed grid snapshots for beat-length loop planning, optional Hot Cue quantization, Beat Jump and bounded one-shot Sync. Master rate is sampled once for Sync so the follower targets the master's effective tempo (`grid BPM × playback rate`) rather than raw source BPM. Manual corrections persist separately from detector output in source-identity-bound records that omit raw path/name payload data. The native deck UI exposes beat-zero and segment-0 BPM controls, reset-to-detector, bounded beat-line overlays and a dedicated reviewed tempo-map dialog. The dialog can add a later boundary at the playhead, select by playhead, move/replace/remove later segments and Undo/Redo a bounded edit history; accepted snapshots persist off callback. Reviewed-grid beat loops, eight persistent Hot Cue pads, Beat Jump, REV/SLIP, bounded JOG/SCRATCH and explicit MASTER/continuous SYNC controls share the same owner boundary.
+
+Continuous Sync performs one bounded initial rate/phase alignment, then re-evaluates reviewed-grid tempo/phase at a bounded message-thread cadence. Rate writes use an epsilon to avoid control churn; phase seeks are suppressed inside a small deadband and rejected outside a stricter correction envelope. Beat Loop and Reverse/Slip ownership release a follower lock, while an active native Jog temporarily suspends maintenance so Sync never fights the platter. This is deterministic control scheduling, not proof of controller timing, hardware latency or arbitrary-music beat-grid accuracy.
 
 A developer-only manifest validator builds with the native app and runs the same detector path on a local user-owned/licensed corpus. It reports stable row IDs, per-reference BPM/key results and aggregate BPM error without printing source paths or requiring the corpus in Git. This makes representative music-domain evidence reproducible, but no corpus result is claimed until it has actually been run and reviewed. See [`docs/ANALYSIS_VALIDATION.md`](docs/ANALYSIS_VALIDATION.md).
 
